===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|linke}}, {{m|enm|lenke}}, from a merger of {{inh|en|ang|hlenċe}}, {{m|ang|hlenċa||ring; chainlink}} and {{der|en|non|*hlenkr}}, {{m|non|hlekkr||ring; chain}}; both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hlankiz||ring; bond; fettle; fetter}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hlankaz|t=bendsome, flexible}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*kleng-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*klenk-|t=to bend; twist; wind}}. Used in English since the 14th century. Related to {{m|en|lank}}.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|nds|Lenk|t=link}}, {{cog|da|lænke|t=chain; link}}, {{cog|ovd|lekk|t=link}}, {{cog|is|hlekkur|t=link}}, {{cog|nb|lenke|t=chain; link}}, {{cog|nn|lenke}}, {{m|nn|lenkje|t=chain; link}}, {{cog|sv|länk|t=chain; link}}.
